WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.870
As much as we want to believe we choose movies
because of intriguing stories instead of A-list

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.630
stars, that often isn't the case.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:10.460
And casting the right person for a role means
choosing an actor who can pull off the character

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:13.480
convincingly, and be bankable enough to sell
lots of tickets.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.190
But Hollywood isn't perfect, and there are
times they get it wrong…or pretty close

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.190
to it.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:21.870
These hit films nearly went in very different
directions with leading roles, only to make

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.730
a clutch casting change, that made them the
blockbusters they are today.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.640
Liam Neeson in Lincoln

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.510
Now that he's starred in three Taken movies,
it can be easy to forget that Liam Neeson

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:35.600
is a serious dramatic actor — with an Oscar
nom for his performance in Schindler's List.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.350
And while his appearance in Life's Too Short
proves he's not nearly as humorless as his

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.670
characters onscreen, sometimes it's the actor
who knows when a part just isn't right.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:47.879
After the success of Schindler's List, director
Steven Spielberg tapped Neeson to lead another

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:50.699
dramatic historical project: Lincoln.

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:53.559
"I like our chances now."

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:56.510
Neeson admitted to GQ, of the table read for
the film:

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:01.109
"I read very, very poorly by any standards,
but then some people come up afterward and

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:03.229
say, 'Oh, you're made to play Lincoln.'

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.140
I just was cringing with embarrassment."

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.120
Ultimately, Neeson stepped away, and the part
went to Daniel Day Lewis, who won an Oscar

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.120
for the role.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.910
"Shall we stop this bleeding?"

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:13.910
Neeson said,

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:17.500
"I was thrilled that Daniel played him, and
when I saw the film, I was like, 'He's f—in'

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.530
Abraham Lincoln.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:19.980
This is perfect.'"

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.040
David Schwimmer in Men in Black

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.240
Men in Black further cemented Will Smith as
one of the biggest movie stars of the '90s,

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.790
adding another hit to an incredible run that
also included Bad Boys and Independence Day.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:34.780
His performance was spot on, in what might
have otherwise been a ridiculous alien flick

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.010
for kids—like if, say, Ross from Friends
had tried to pull it off.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:41.150
"You know what the difference is between you
and me?

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.500
I make this look good."

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.060
David Schwimmer was offered the role, but
turned it down because he was already starring

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.510
in and directing the dramedy Since You've
Been Gone.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:53.510
If you feel bad for Schwimmer missing out
on a colossal payday, remember that Smith

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.500
passed on playing Neo in The Matrix to play
the lead in Wild Wild West.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.280
We all make mistakes.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.500
Frank Sinatra in Dirty Harry

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.060
Until a few years ago, the rumor that Frank
Sinatra was cast to play the title role in

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.560
the 1971 cop drama Dirty Harry was just that—a
rumor.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.319
But in 2015, Academy Award-winning director
William Friedkin confirmed on Alec Baldwin's

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:18.830
podcast Here's the Thing that before Eastwood,
it was Ol' Blue Eyes.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:25.780
"My producer, a guy named Phil D'Antoni, he
and I were going to do Dirty Harry with Frank

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.230
Sinatra. [...] and then Sinatra pulled out."

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.819
According to Yahoo! Movies, film critic Ty
Burr's book, Gods Like Us, explained Sinatra's

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:34.819
reasoning as:

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:38.849
"A broken wrist sustained during The Manchurian
Candidate eight years earlier meant that Old

00:02:38.849 --> 00:02:41.849
Blue Eyes couldn't hold the heavy Magnum pistol
comfortably."

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:46.670
Far better known as a singer, Sinatra earned
acclaim as an actor, but tough-guy parts weren't

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.990
exactly his forte.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:53.159
"You've got to ask yourself one question:
Do I feel lucky?

00:02:53.159 --> 00:02:55.040
Well do ya, punk?"

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.010
Britney Spears in The Notebook

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.439
The movie that launched Ryan Gosling's movie
career almost never happened.

00:03:00.439 --> 00:03:04.439
The Notebook hit a major hiccup during filming
when Gosling asked the director to bring in

00:03:04.439 --> 00:03:07.840
a different actress because he wasn't getting
along with costar Rachel McAdams.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.180
"Wait a minute.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.810
We're not really breakin' up are we?

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:15.799
Come on, this is just a fight we're having
and tomorrow it'll be like it never happened,

00:03:15.799 --> 00:03:16.799
right?"

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:17.800
Director Nick Cassavetes told VH1,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.530
"He's doing a scene with Rachel and he says,
'Would you take her out of here and bring

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.620
in another actress to read off-camera with
me?

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:24.829
[...] I can't do it with her.

00:03:24.829 --> 00:03:26.409
I'm just not getting anything from this.'

00:03:26.409 --> 00:03:29.980
We went into a room with a producer; they
started screaming and yelling at each other.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:30.980
I walked out."

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:34.970
In the end, Gosling and McAdams shelved their
differences, but before they made up, Gosling

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:38.749
screen-tested with an old friend from The
Mickey Mouse Club: Britney Spears.

00:03:38.749 --> 00:03:41.129
Gosling confirmed Spears was up for the role,
telling ET,

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:45.670
"I hadn't seen her really since she was about
12—we were both 12—so she's grown up,

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.310
but she was really good, actually."

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:51.599
Pleasantries aside, the driving force behind
The Notebook's success was the acting, and

00:03:51.599 --> 00:03:55.230
an inexperienced Spears likely couldn't have
carried the role.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.260
Molly Ringwald in Pretty Woman

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:01.749
When it came time to cast Pretty Woman, production
had to choose between two incredible actors.

00:04:01.749 --> 00:04:06.719
Responding to questions on a Reddit AMA, Molly
Ringwald admitted she was once under consideration

00:04:06.719 --> 00:04:08.400
for the lead role in the movie.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.469
With credits like The Breakfast Club and Pretty
in Pink to her name, few could contend that

00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:14.599
Ringwald wouldn't have done a fantastic job.

00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:18.250
But Julia Roberts ended up winning the part—and
walking away with the movie.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.340
"You are, in fact, a hooker, and you are my
employee."

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.340
"Look you don't own me.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:23.340
I decide, okay?

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.030
I say who, I say when, I say who!"

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:27.620
Even Ringwald agrees, saying,

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.699
"Julia Roberts is what makes that movie.

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:30.699
It was her part.

00:04:30.699 --> 00:04:33.870
Every actor hopes for a part that lets them
shine like that."

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.289
Tom Selleck in Raiders of the Lost Ark

00:04:36.289 --> 00:04:40.949
George Lucas, who co-wrote the Steven Spielberg-directed
Raiders of the Lost Ark, didn't want Harrison

00:04:40.949 --> 00:04:42.900
Ford to star as Indiana Jones.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.259
In a making-of featurette, Spielberg remembered,

00:04:45.259 --> 00:04:51.319
"I had just seen a cut of Empire Strikes Back
and said to George, that's Indiana Jones right

00:04:51.319 --> 00:04:52.319
up there.

00:04:52.319 --> 00:04:53.319
Han Solo.

00:04:53.319 --> 00:04:54.319
Why can't he do it?"

00:04:54.319 --> 00:04:55.349
And Lucas told Spielberg,

00:04:55.349 --> 00:04:58.259
"He's identified as Han Solo, let's leave
him as Han Solo.

00:04:58.259 --> 00:05:00.220
And I said yeah but he's an actor.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:02.100
Actors play hundreds of different characters."

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:06.640
Ford was cast, but only as a replacement for
their first choice: Tom Selleck, who had already

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:07.990
been cast as Indy.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:11.740
Selleck told David Letterman that CBS made
him turn down the role, in favor of their

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:12.740
show, Magnum, P.I.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:18.849
[Theme music from Magnum P.I.]

00:05:18.849 --> 00:05:19.849
Selleck told Letterman,

00:05:19.849 --> 00:05:24.569
"The more they held out the offer and talked
to the network, the more the network said

00:05:24.569 --> 00:05:25.879
no."

00:05:25.879 --> 00:05:30.490
"The network of course being CBS."

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:34.680
But at this point, it's impossible to imagine
anyone but Ford cracking that whip.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.930
Eric Stoltz in Back to the Future

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:41.540
Michael J. Fox, who was booked solid at the
time with his hit NBC sitcom Family Ties,

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:45.060
came very close to not getting his big screen
breakout in Back To The Future.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:49.990
Forced to go without his first pick for leading
man, director Robert Zemeckis originally cast

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.000
Eric Stoltz in the role of Marty McFly.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.449
"He's an absolute dream."

00:05:54.449 --> 00:05:58.379
Production began with Stoltz playing Marty,
but the crew knew something just wasn't right,

00:05:58.379 --> 00:06:03.000
and Zemeckis worked out a deal with NBC for
Fox to film around his Family Ties schedule

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.150
and replace Stoltz—who'd already filmed
a number of scenes.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:07.229
Stoltz took it hard.

00:06:07.229 --> 00:06:11.650
But in 2016, eagle-eyed fans noticed something:
Stoltz made the final cut.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:15.509
In the scene when Marty punches Biff in the
diner, that's Stoltz throwing the punch.

00:06:15.509 --> 00:06:16.889
Thanks for watching!

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:19.670
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:21.889
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

